{"skill":{"slug":"openclaw-config","displayName":"OpenClaw Config"},"version":{"version":"0.1.0","createdAt":1770453145213,"changelog":"- Initial release of openclaw-config skill.\n- Edit and validate OpenClaw Gateway config (openclaw.json / JSON5) with schema-first workflow.\n- Guides users through safe config editing, strict validation, and use of $include for modular configuration.\n- Includes best practices, troubleshooting steps, and common configuration examples for channels, tools, and security.\n- Comprehensive documentation for locating schemas, resolving validation errors, and maintaining strict security policies.","changelogSource":"auto","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":4722,"sha256":"a4e909eb8e266b0d7aab781ee1e1b30054bc939d0289a0792e43b10b0f910504","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"agents/openai.yaml","size":107,"sha256":"790a823fde186d047f5e538d18583b0b69313f5da8c09ba53f58bccac944b167","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"references/openclaw-config-fields.md","size":4684,"sha256":"3888d560f9c21c527965e692c399b616a3dfe2404fbf6973e061324c00ab1deb","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"references/schema-sources.md","size":2864,"sha256":"dfcaf7058a53f990b489b255fc7ed00b82a98fc3975f03a2b4edcef4328a89b8","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/openclaw-config-check.sh","size":1422,"sha256":"3923183abeb1ef689beabd89d1d0037e4f64f3bbff2ff479d8ac905f197ddd9e","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2136,"sha256":"f0edd02998e3082f219a1b2a28e37f587cc3791c6839e1fa71daf52a17e3184a","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"clean","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789037835693,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"4d313c655fa3c83252f141d134c506d8090f9502baa845d8df36d76627b7021f","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/4d313c655fa3c83252f141d134c506d8090f9502baa845d8df36d76627b7021f","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: openclaw-config\nVersion: 0.1.0\n\nThe skill bundle is benign, focusing on safe editing and validation of OpenClaw Gateway configuration. Key indicators of benign intent include explicit guardrails in SKILL.md against automatically applying fixes (`openclaw doctor --fix/--yes`) without user consent, strong recommendations to use environment variables for secrets instead of committing them to config, and a shell script (`scripts/openclaw-config-check.sh`) that performs security-conscious file permission checks on the configuration file. There is no evidence of data exfiltration, malicious execution, or prompt injection attempts to subvert the agent's purpose.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779161990903},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":36,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":5,"checkedAt":1789037835693},"llm":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","confidence":"high","summary":"This is a coherent OpenClaw configuration helper that can change persistent gateway settings, but its behavior is disclosed, scoped to that purpose, and not deceptive.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Access to ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json, OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH, the openclaw CLI, and config schema RPC is proportionate for an OpenClaw config skill, though users should treat resulting config changes as security-sensitive.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The artifact contains Markdown references, a small YAML display file, and a diagnostic shell script; no install hook, auto-run behavior, dependency installation, or remote payload execution was found.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The skill covers high-impact config areas such as gateway auth, tools, skills, plugins, and channels, but it frames changes as user-directed config edits and includes guardrails such as preferring small edits and requiring explicit consent for doctor auto-fix behavior.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Config edits are persistent by nature and may restart or affect the Gateway, but this is disclosed and purpose-aligned; no cron jobs, startup hooks, sudo execution, backdoors, or hidden persistence mechanisms were found.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill's stated purpose is to edit and validate OpenClaw Gateway config, and the instructions focus on schema lookup, small config edits, validation, and config references.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"ok"}],"guidance":"Install only if you want an agent to help inspect and change OpenClaw Gateway configuration. Review proposed config changes before applying them, avoid storing long-lived secrets directly in openclaw.json, and be especially careful with plugin, skill, auth, remote access, and tool-execution settings.","findings":"[LP3] expected: Use of OpenClaw CLI and config RPC commands is expected for a config-editing skill. The artifact does not declare a formal allowed-tools boundary, but the instructions are narrowed to OpenClaw config/schema/doctor operations rather than broad shell use.\n[RA2] expected: The cited text describes config.patch/config.apply and optional Gateway restart as part of applying config changes, not unauthorized session persistence. No cron, startup script, or background persistence behavior was found.\n[SQP-2] expected: The skills/plugins reference lists valid OpenClaw config fields for loading and installs; it does not instruct users to install arbitrary untrusted extensions. A clearer trust warning would improve guidance but does not make the skill suspicious.\n[PE2] expected: The script prints a recommendation to consider chmod 600 when config permissions are loose, but it does not execute chmod, sudo, or root commands. The finding is downgraded as a false positive for privilege escalation.\n[SQP-2] expected: The auth and remote config fields are expected in a field index. The main skill separately advises preferring environment variables or credential files and avoiding committed long-lived tokens, though a localized warning near those fields would be useful.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789037835693}}}}}